eferences and theme preview" width="1240" height="488" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><strong>AmigaOS · Appearance</strong><span>Choose a shared theme and preview it before saving.</span></figcaption></figure><figure><a href="assets/screenshots/amiga-network.png"><img src="assets/screenshots/amiga-network.png" alt="AmigaOS network preferences with visibility and transfer limits" width="1240" height="488" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><strong>AmigaOS · Network controls</strong><span>Control visibility, incoming transfers and send/receive limits.</span></figcaption></figure><figure><a href="assets/screenshots/amiga-about.png"><img src="assets/screenshots/amiga-about.png" alt="AmigaOS About page with project links and ARexx information" width="1240" height="488" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><strong>AmigaOS · About BitSkim</strong><span>Project links, release information and ARexx integration.</span></figcaption></figure><figure><a href="assets/screenshots/dos-files.png"><img src="assets/screenshots/dos-files.png" alt="DOS file browser with tagged-send controls" width="1440" height="800" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><strong>DOS · File browser</strong><span>Browse local folders and tag multiple files for sending.</span></figcaption></figure></div></details></div></section>
<section id="get" class="wrap section get"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">MAKE THE CONNECTION</p><h2>Find your<br>machine.</h2></div><p>BitSkim is free to download. Both computers need BitSkim and a reachable IPv4 connection. Choose a client below.</p></div><p class="download-status"><?php if ($releaseCatalog['source'] !== null): ?>Downloads are selected from published GitHub releases; each client shows its available version.<?php else: ?>Showing GitHub release 0.1 downloads; automatic updates are temporarily unavailable. <a href="https://github.com/retrocs-org/bitskim/releases">Check GitHub releases ↗</a>.<?php endif; ?></p><h3 class="client-group-title">Available clients</h3><div class="client-grid"><?php bitskim_client_card('macOS', 'Available', 'Apple silicon · macOS 13+ · signed and notarized', [['mac-dmg','Disk image','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-macOS-arm64.dmg'],['mac-zip','Portable ZIP','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-macOS-arm64.zip']]); ?><?php bitskim_client_card('Windows', 'Available', 'Windows 10/11 x64 target · unsigned · interactive setup testing pending', [['windows-setup','Setup','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-Windows10-x64-Setup.exe'],['windows-zip','Portable ZIP','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-Windows10-x64.zip']]); ?><?php bitskim_client_card('AmigaOS', 'Available', 'AmigaOS 3.x · 68000 · Workbench · TCP/IP stack required', [['amiga-lha','LHA','downloads/BitSkim-0.1.lha'],['amiga-zip','ZIP','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-AmigaOS.zip']]); ?></div><h3 class="client-group-title">Experimental clients</h3><div class="client-grid"><?php bitskim_client_card('DOS / mTCP', 'Experimental', '8086 · packet driver required · GPLv3+ with source · hardware coverage pending', [['dos-zip','Client ZIP','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-DOS-mTCP.zip']]); ?><?php bitskim_client_card('RISC OS', 'Experimental', 'RISC OS 3.x · APCS-26 · TCP/IP required · compiled, not runtime tested', [['riscos-arm2','ARM2 ZIP','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-RISCOS3-ARM2.zip'],['riscos-strongarm','StrongARM ZIP','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-RISCOS3-StrongARM.zip']]); ?><?php bitskim_client_card('Atari TOS / MiNTNet', 'Experimental', 'STinG / MiNTNet · startup checked · transfers unverified', [['atari-sting','TOS / STinG','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-Atari-TOS-STinG-68000.zip'],['atari-mintnet','MiNTNet 68000','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-Atari-MiNTNet-68000.zip'],['atari-mintnet060','MiNTNet 68060','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-Atari-MiNTNet-68060.zip']]); ?><?php bitskim_client_card('Windows Classic', 'Experimental', 'Windows 95/98/Me/XP target · unsigned · runtime validation pending', [['windows-classic','Portable x86 ZIP','downloads/BitSkim-0.1-Windows-Classic-x86.zip']]); ?></div><details class="planned-clients"><summary>In development and planned · not released</summary><div class="client-grid"><article class="client-card"><div class="client-heading"><h3>iPhone / iPad</h3><span class="pill muted">In development</span></div><span class="client-version">Not released</span><p>Experimental iOS 16+ source-only client. No App Store or TestFlight download yet.</p><div class="client-links"><a href="https://github.com/retrocs-org/bitskim/blob/main/docs/IOS.md">Development guide ↗</a></div></article><article class="client-card"><div class="client-heading"><h3>Windows 3.1</h3><span class="pill muted">Planned</span></div><span class="client-version">Not released</span><p>No client download yet.</p><div class="client-links"></div></article><article class="client-card"><div class="client-heading"><h3>Linux</h3><span class="pill muted">Planned</span></div><span class="client-version">Not released</span><p>No client download yet.</p><div class="client-links"></div></article></div></details><p class="catalog-note">Experimental builds have limited validation; compiling a client does not establish hardware compatibility. <a href="release-notes.php">Platform details ↗</a></p><div class="source-line"><a href="https://github.com/retrocs-org/bitskim">Browse the source ↗</a><a href="<?= site_escape(bitskim_source_url()) ?>" download>Source archive</a><a href="#build">Build from source</a><a href="https://github.com/retrocs-org/bitskim/releases">All releases ↗</a><a href="<?= site_escape($releaseCatalog['source'] !== null ? $releaseCatalog['release_url'] : 'release-notes.php') ?>">Release notes</a></div></section>
<section id="build" class="wrap section build-source"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">MAKE IT YOUR OWN</p><h2>Build from source.</h2></div><p>Compile a client directly with the supplied platform scripts. Use Python 3.9+, a POSIX shell and the target toolchain; run commands from the project folder.</p></div><details class="build-guide"><summary>Requirements and manual build commands</summary><h3>Get the source</h3><p>Extract the <a href="<?= site_escape(bitskim_source_url()) ?>" download>source archive</a>, or clone the repository:</p><pre><code>git clone https://github.com/retrocs-org/bitskim.git
cd bitskim</code></pre><p>Modern Mac and Windows builds also need Perl, make, curl and initial download access for pinned OpenSSL. Install toolchains separately and export their paths in your shell; standalone commands do not load your external release settings. Generated icons and themes are included.</p><div class="build-platforms"><article><h3>macOS · Apple silicon</h3><p>Xcode command-line tools/macOS SDK, Clang and Swift. Targets macOS 13+.</p><pre><code>./scripts/build-macos.sh</code></pre><p>Output: <code>build/macos/BitSkim.app</code>.</p></article><article><h3>Windows · x64</h3><p>MinGW-w64 GCC, windres and binutils in PATH; run from a POSIX shell. The first OpenSSL build requires the x86_64-w64-mingw32 tools in PATH, even if compiler variables are set.</p><pre><code>./scripts/build-windows.sh</code></pre><p>Output: <code>build/windows/BitSkim.exe</code>.</p></article><article><h3>AmigaOS · 68000</h3><p>AmigaPorts GCC, NDK and libnix with nix20 support.</p><pre><code>AMIGA_CC=/path/to/m68k-amigaos-gcc ./scripts/build-amiga.sh</code></pre><p>Output: <code>build/amiga/BitSkim</code>.</p></article></div><details class="manual-retro"><summary>Experimental clients · manual build commands</summary><div class="build-platforms"><article><h3>iPhone / iPad · in development</h3><p>Apple silicon Mac, full Xcode with iOS SDKs, XcodeGen, Perl, make and curl. iOS 16+ target. Manual connections by default; discovery requires Apple entitlement approval.</p><pre><code>./scripts/build-ios.sh iphoneos</code></pre><p>Unsigned development app; device signing and App Group provisioning are needed to install. <a href="https://github.com/retrocs-org/bitskim/blob/main/docs/IOS.md">iOS development guide ↗</a>.</p></article><article><h3>Atari · TOS / MiNTNet</h3><p>Compatible m68k-atari-mint GCC with MiNTLib/GEMlib and STinG headers. Builds all three variants.</p><pre><code>ATARI_CC=/path/to/m68k-atari-mint-gcc STING_INCLUDE=/path/to/sting-sdk/include ./scripts/build-atari.sh</code></pre><p>Output: <code>build/atari/{sting,mintnet,mintnet060}/BITSKIM.PRG</code>.</p></article><article><h3>RISC OS · ARM2 / StrongARM</h3><p>Working legacy GCCSDK 2.95.4 AOF/UnixLib, APCS-26. Builds both CPU variants.</p><pre><code>RISCOS_CC=/path/to/legacy/gcc ./scripts/build-riscos.sh</code></pre><p>Output: <code>build/riscos/{arm2,strongarm}/!BitSkim</code>.</p></article><article><h3>DOS · 8086 / mTCP</h3><p>Open Watcom 16-bit DOS tools and a configured DOS build runner. The guide also describes the unvalidated Windows-native route.</p><pre><code>python3 scripts/build-dos.py --watcom /path/to/open-watcom --runner /path/to/dos-runner</code></pre><p>Output: <code>build/dos/BITSKIM.EXE</code>.</p></article><article><h3>Windows Classic · x86</h3><p>i686 MinGW-w64 GCC and windres in a POSIX shell, with the libmsvcrt-os.a import library. Runtime validation remains pending.</p><pre><code>LEGACY_WINDOWS_CC=/path/to/i686-w64-mingw32-gcc LEGACY_WINDOWS_RC=/path/to/i686-w64-mingw32-windres ./scripts/build-windows-legacy.sh</code></pre><p>Output: <code>build/windows-legacy/BitSkim.exe</code>.</p></article></div></details><h3>Check the shared engine</h3><p>On macOS/Linux, provide a host C compiler named <code>cc</code> and local socket access. Application and hardware testing remain separate.</p><pre><code>python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -b</code></pre><h3>Create a Windows installer</h3><p>After building the Windows client, provide NSIS 3 and its matching Stubs/Include/Plugins data:</p><pre><code>MAKENSIS=/path/to/nsis/bin/makensis NSISDIR=/path/to/nsis/share/nsis ./scripts/build-windows-installer.sh</code></pre><p>Finish source, documentation and website edits before compiling: packaging rejects build records after any of those files change. Rebuild affected package targets after edits.</p><p>The <a href="https://github.com/retrocs-org/bitskim/blob/main/docs/BUILDING.md">complete manual build guide ↗</a> includes target outputs, Atari SDK preparation, Mac disk images and local package creation. It is also included as <code>docs/BUILDING.md</code> in the source archive.</p></details></section>

<section id="support" class="wrap section faq"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">HELP &amp; ANSWERS</p><h2>Support.</h2></div><p>Getting connected, choosing a client, and answers to common questions.</p></div><details class="faq-disclosure"><summary>Frequently asked questions</summary><div class="faq-answers"><details><summary>What does the Amiga need?</summary><p>AmigaOS 3.x, a Workbench screen at least 640×256, and an AmiTCP-compatible TCP/IP stack exposing bsdsocket.library version 4.</p></details><details><summary>What can version 0.1 send?</summary><p>Files up to 2 GiB minus one byte each, plus nested folders and empty directories on capable clients. Queues, conflict choices, speed limits and session history help manage a transfer. Filesystem metadata is not preserved. See the release notes for feature and platform limits.</p></details><details><summary>Does this work with AirDrop or over the Internet?</summary><p>BitSkim uses its own protocol; both computers need the app. Version 0.1 is intended for trusted local networks. Keep its listening port off the public Internet.</p></details><details><summary>How do computers find each other?</summary><p>Open BitSkim on both computers and enable visibility and receiving. BitSkim sends UDP broadcasts on the local network to discover nearby clients automatically. No account or central server is needed.</p></details><details><summary>Why is the other computer missing?</summary><p>Check that both apps are running, receiving is enabled, and both computers have a reachable IPv4 connection. Guest Wi-Fi, client isolation, routers and VPNs can block broadcasts. Try the other computer’s IPv4 address directly; this still requires a reachable network connection.</p></details><details><summary>Which network ports does BitSkim use?</summary><p>Discovery uses UDP port 46440. The default TCP listening port is also 46440, although a client can advertise a different listening port. Allow the app through your firewall on your trusted local network. Port forwarding is not needed for local transfers.</p></details><details><summary>Are transfers encrypted?</summary><p>Mac and modern Windows clients prefer TLS encryption. Retro connections use plaintext. To verify a modern device’s identity, compare verification codes before remembering its public key. Use BitSkim on a trusted local network. <a href="release-notes.php">Read the security details.</a></p></details><details><summary>Can a modern computer send files to a classic one?</summary><p>Yes, when both computers run compatible BitSkim clients and can reach each other over IPv4. Features depend on the receiving client. An experimental build is available to try, but its label does not guarantee compatibility with every machine or network stack.</p></details><details><summary>Will my original filenames and file metadata survive?</summary><p>BitSkim maps filenames when needed for the destination filesystem. Version 0.1 does not preserve permissions, timestamps, Mac resource forks or retro filetype metadata. Keep an archive of the original files when that information matters.</p></details><details><summary>What does Experimental mean?</summary><p>These clients have limited runtime or hardware validation. Their download cards and release notes describe the checks completed so far. Planned clients have not been released and are listed separately.</p></details><details><summary>Is there an iPhone or iPad client?</summary><p>An experimental iOS client is in development, with file sharing and foreground receiving. It is source-only, with no public iOS download yet. <a href="https://github.com/retrocs-org/bitskim/blob/main/docs/IOS.md">Read the development guide ↗</a>.</p></details><details><summary>Can I build BitSkim myself?</summary><p>Yes. One shared source archive contains the project and build instructions. See <a href="index.php#build">Build from source</a> for requirements and commands, including a link to the complete platform guide.</p></details></div></details></section>
